<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Beloozero</h1><p class="st-meta">1238 CE – 1389 CE (approx.) · capital Beloozero</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A northern principality on the White Lake, gateway to the fur trade, whose last princes died at Kulikovo before Moscow took it over.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belozersk">Beloozero</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-beloye">White Lake</a>, is mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> under 862 as the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik">Rurik's</a> brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sineus">Sineus</a>, in the land of the Finnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veps">Ves</a>. Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> lands, it became a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> in 1238 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gleb-vasilkovich">Gleb Vasilkovich</a>, son of the Rostov prince killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality was a remote, thinly settled land of forests and lakes on the routes to the north, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, fish and salt were gathered. Its princes depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a>, the grand princes of Vladimir and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyodor-romanovich-of-beloozero">Fyodor Romanovich</a> and his son were killed fighting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo">Kulikovo</a> in 1380.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After their deaths <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> took control, and in his will of 1389 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belozersk">Beloozero</a> to his son Andrei. In 1397 the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-of-white-lake">Kirill</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirillo-belozersky-monastery">Kirillo-Belozersky Monastery</a> nearby, which became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia's</a> richest monasteries.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-beloozero.jpg" alt="Principality of Beloozero" width="320" height="289"><figcaption>Image: Hellerick · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rostov_Yaroslavl_Beloozero_Galich_northern_Vladimir_14th_century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1238 CE – 1389 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Beloozero</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gleb Vasilkovich, Fyodor Romanovich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Belozersk Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1314&amp;focus=principality-of-beloozero" aria-label="Principality of Beloozero on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5472 167 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Beloozero lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5792 423L5878 523M5792 423L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5878 523h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5792 423h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5792 423h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1314&amp;focus=principality-of-beloozero">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
